  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nCurrent upper level pattern shows low pressure moving east across\nsouthern Canada. This will result in a favorable pattern for gap \nwinds for the foothills west of Billings (Livingston and Nye \nareas) today. Wind gusts are expected to remain in the 35-50 mph \nrange as a modest gradient forms and 700 mb winds remain weak \n(20-25 kts). Into Thursday afternoon and Friday, a shortwave \nmoving across northern MT while under a mostly westerly flow will \nwork to tighten the gradient further. As 700 mb winds also \nincrease to 35-50 kts, this will increase winds from Livingston to\nBig Timber and Harlowton. Currently, there is about a 50% chance \nfor winds to reach 60 mph at Big Timber and Harlowton, with about \na 70% chance for Livingston late Thursday into Friday. Make sure \nto plan ahead as travel may become hazardous Thursday evening into\nFriday, especially for high profile vehicles. As this shortwave \napproaches, precipitation chances increase across the higher \nterrain of south central MT. Confidence is high for mountain snow \nwith up to a 80% chance for at least 12 inches of snow for the \nAbsaroka/Beartooth mountains Thursday night through Saturday. \nSimilar chances exist for the Bighorn and Crazy mountains for at \nleast 6 inches of snow. Models are currently not bringing a lot of\ncold air with this system, so as of now, much of the plains will \nsee a rain/snow mix. This could certainly change as it still is a \nways out. \n\nTemperatures are expected to gradually warm through Friday with\nhighs reaching the 40s to low 50s. Slightly cooler conditions will\nfollow this end of week system, lowering temperatures into the \nhigh 30s and 40s. TS",
